WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.550
A plan describes the schedule you authored.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:09.945
A progress forecast asks what current progress and
remaining-work assumptions imply about the future.

00:00:10.065 --> 00:00:13.915
A scenario explores an alternative before you commit it.

00:00:14.035 --> 00:00:17.285
This lesson keeps those three ideas separate.

00:00:17.405 --> 00:00:24.230
We will fix a status date, inspect the forecast,
investigate a delay and create a named alternative.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:31.700
You should finish able to explain the assumptions behind a
proposed change and review its effect before applying it
to the plan.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.250
Expand Diagram options and turn Progress forecast on.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:45.045
Select the sixteenth of November as the status date so
that the example is repeatable.

00:00:45.165 --> 00:00:52.415
Automatic Today uses the document's forecast time zone,
while an explicit date gives a fixed point of reference.

00:00:52.535 --> 00:00:56.910
Read the status date alongside the task progress values.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:04.580
A forecast based on an inappropriate date can be
misleading even if every calculation is internally
consistent.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:09.225
Check that enabling this view does not rewrite the
authored task statements.

00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:15.708
Compare the planned and forecast bars.

00:01:15.828 --> 00:01:23.778
The forecast is not a record that the plan was already
changed; it is a calculation based on the current status
and assumptions.

00:01:23.898 --> 00:01:27.123
Follow Backend and the work that depends on it.

00:01:27.243 --> 00:01:32.043
A delayed predecessor can affect later tasks and the
launch milestone.

00:01:32.163 --> 00:01:37.513
Keep the dependency chain visible instead of reading only
one finish date.

00:01:37.633 --> 00:01:44.658
Before discussing an action, identify which result belongs
to the authored plan and which belongs to the forecast.

00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:53.167
Use the task and forecast explanations to investigate a
changed finish.

00:01:53.287 --> 00:01:59.512
Low completion, remaining effort, allocation, closed days
and dependencies can all matter.

00:01:59.632 --> 00:02:08.607
Backend has substantial remaining work and a half-time
assignment in this example, so a simple calendar glance is
not enough to explain delivery.

00:02:08.727 --> 00:02:13.302
Inspect the task's progress and resource assumptions
before changing them.

00:02:13.422 --> 00:02:21.722
A useful forecast discussion names the cause and the
assumption, rather than treating the projected date as an
unexplained prediction.

00:02:25.233 --> 00:02:27.958
Choose What-if scenario from Plan.

00:02:28.078 --> 00:02:34.228
The base plan is a snapshot used for comparison; the draft
is where you explore an alternative.

00:02:34.348 --> 00:02:38.423
Changing the draft does not immediately change the current
plan.

00:02:38.543 --> 00:02:43.318
Inspect the task controls and the comparison modes before
editing.

00:02:43.438 --> 00:02:51.813
Keep the alternative small enough to explain: for example,
change Backend's effort or assignment assumption and
observe the consequence.

00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:58.208
A scenario is useful when its assumptions are explicit,
not just when it produces an earlier finish.

00:03:01.733 --> 00:03:05.033
Select Backend in the scenario task picker.

00:03:05.153 --> 00:03:11.828
Set its effort to eight days and choose Update scenario to
include the task-control edits in the draft.

00:03:11.948 --> 00:03:14.598
Compare that result with the base.

00:03:14.718 --> 00:03:23.518
An earlier result is not automatically a better plan:
reducing effort requires a reason, such as reduced scope
or a changed estimate.

00:03:23.638 --> 00:03:28.313
Resource changes likewise need a realistic availability
assumption.

00:03:28.433 --> 00:03:33.608
Inspect warnings and the source patch before treating the
alternative as a decision.

00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:41.633
Give the alternative a meaningful name and record why it
might work.

00:03:41.753 --> 00:03:48.303
We will call this one Reduced backend scope and state that
the estimate assumes a smaller release boundary.

00:03:48.423 --> 00:03:50.948
Save it as a new scenario.

00:03:51.068 --> 00:03:56.893
Named alternatives let you compare proposals without
confusing them with the live schedule.

00:03:57.013 --> 00:04:06.213
Use the saved-scenario controls to reopen or copy an
alternative later, and keep its base and assumptions
visible when the current plan has moved on.

00:04:09.733 --> 00:04:14.158
Save a named eight day back end alternative with its
assumptions.

00:04:14.278 --> 00:04:21.178
Set the copied alternative to nine days, change its name
and save it as a new scenario against the same base.

00:04:21.298 --> 00:04:27.048
Reopen the original saved alternative and confirm its
eight day task estimate.

00:04:27.168 --> 00:04:32.368
Now close the draft and change the current plan to eleven
days for that task.

00:04:32.488 --> 00:04:39.763
Reopen the saved alternative and review it against the
changed plan, paying attention to the stale base guidance.

00:04:39.883 --> 00:04:45.133
Keep the current plan unchanged while deciding how to
reconcile the alternative.

00:04:45.253 --> 00:04:51.678
Choose Reconcile with current plan and inspect the
overlapping current and scenario task estimates.

00:04:51.798 --> 00:04:57.673
Explicitly keep the scenario version of the task, then
choose Use reconciled scenario.

00:04:57.793 --> 00:05:07.743
Review the updated source patch; the rebased alternative
remains a proposal, and the authored plan still contains
eleven days until you apply a reviewed result.

00:05:11.267 --> 00:05:16.517
Choose Review and apply to inspect the proposed source
changes and schedule impact.

00:05:16.637 --> 00:05:22.712
Confirm that the draft changes only what you intended and
that reported problems are resolved.

00:05:22.832 --> 00:05:29.432
Apply scenario commits the reviewed result to the current
plan; closing an uncommitted draft is different.

00:05:29.552 --> 00:05:37.577
If the plan or capacities changed since the saved base,
reconcile the alternative rather than applying a stale
proposal blindly.

00:05:37.697 --> 00:05:41.447
Recheck the actual source after an accepted change.

00:05:44.967 --> 00:05:50.167
Enable the forecast and set its status date before opening
Apply to plan.

00:05:50.287 --> 00:05:53.837
Review the proposed task dates and source changes.

00:05:53.957 --> 00:05:57.657
Opening the review leaves the authored plan unchanged.

00:05:57.777 --> 00:06:07.152
Accept Apply to plan in this disposable example, verify
that the source changes and the preview is current, then
save a portable copy of the result.

00:06:07.272 --> 00:06:12.097
Applying a forecast is a separate decision from enabling
its overlay.

00:06:12.217 --> 00:06:16.842
Keep a recoverable checkpoint before committing a
significant reschedule.

00:06:20.367 --> 00:06:24.267
Pause and set the fixed status date in the supplied plan.

00:06:24.387 --> 00:06:31.437
Identify one task that drives a delayed result and explain
its remaining-work and allocation assumptions.

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:36.157
Create two alternatives with different, realistic
justifications.

00:06:36.277 --> 00:06:42.202
Name and save them, review each patch and choose only the
one whose assumptions you can defend.

00:06:42.322 --> 00:06:51.447
You have succeeded when you can distinguish the original
plan, the forecast and the selected draft, and identify
exactly what applying it would change.

00:06:54.967 --> 00:06:59.217
Use forecasting to make assumptions and consequences
visible.

00:06:59.337 --> 00:07:05.812
Use scenarios to explore alternatives, and review source
changes before they become the current plan.

00:07:05.932 --> 00:07:11.407
Keep status dates, time zones, remaining work and resource
capacity explicit.

00:07:11.527 --> 00:07:22.052
The reporting lesson uses these same facts to prepare
useful summaries and check-ins, while retaining the
distinction between a calculation, an observation and a
decision.
